{"name":"Artichoke Dip","hex":"#a19676","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"artichoke","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A plant related to the thistle with enlarged flower heads eaten as a vegetable while immature, Cynara cardunculus var. scolymus.","A dull green colour, like that of an artichoke."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/artichoke"},{"term":"dip","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Northeastern Dinka."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A lower section of a road or geological feature.","Inclination downward; direction below a horizontal line; slope; pitch."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/dip"}],"entries":[{"title":"Artichoke dip","blocks":[{"text":"Artichoke dip is a dip that uses artichoke as a primary ingredient. Some versions are served chunky, while others are puréed and have a smooth texture. It may be served as an hors d'oeuvre along with crackers or chips for dipping. Commercially prepared artichoke dips are produced for consumer purchase. It has been described as \"one of the most popular appetizer menu items of all time\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Artichoke_dip.jpg/330px-Artichoke_dip.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artichoke_dip","wikidata":"Q25041745"},{"title":"Artichoke","blocks":[{"text":"The artichoke is a variety of a species of thistle cultivated as food."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Artichoke_J1.jpg/330px-Artichoke_J1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artichoke","wikidata":"Q23041430"},{"title":"Shades of chartreuse","blocks":[{"text":"Shades of chartreuse are listed below. Chartreuse is a color between yellow and green, so named because of its resemblance to the color of the French liqueur green ch